  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Today and Thursday will be the warmest days before the change\n   back to fall-like weather. \n\n-  Choppy lake conditions Thursday due to westerly winds between\n   15 and 25 mph, and locally higher speeds expected over the \n   Divide.\n\n-  Stiff northeast winds are possible Friday night through \n   Saturday...choppy lakes expected.\n\n-  Cutoff Low brings widespread rain, with snow above 7000 feet \n   Saturday night into Sunday. \n   \n\nThe satellite imagery this morning reveals fog that has developed\nin several valleys from the US-12 corridor west of Lolo Pass, to\nnorthwest Montana. Expect any fog to burn off by mid-morning\ntoday. It's going to feel like summer again as highs will rise\ninto the 70s most of our forecast area with the exception of the\nlower valleys of central Idaho where 80s are expected.\n\nA weakening moisture plume will shift south out of Canada later\ntonight and will be over northwest Montana by Thursday morning.\nThe forcing is relative weak, but couldn't rule out a few light\nshowers in the mountains from Yaak to Glacier National Park around\ndaybreak. Other than the increase in clouds, Thursday will\ngenerally be a warm, dry and breezy day across the region.\nWesterly winds will pick up in the afternoon, generally 10 to 15\nmph with gusts to 25 mph, higher gusts over the Divide. \n\nBy Thursday night, a weak front will become the impetus for\nshowers that continue throughout the day Friday. A northeast wind\ngradient develops by Friday evening which will enhance shower\nactivity over the Glacier Region, and also could cause Lake\nMacDonald to become choppy. \n\nAn impressive 15 millibar easterly wind/pressure gradient sets up\non Saturday in response to the incoming low pressure system moving\nonshore over Oregon. There is a 50% chance that sustained winds \ncould exceed 15 mph and gusts exceed 25 mph over Flathead Lake. \nOther areas, especially channeled locations could also experience \nelevated winds.\n\nA cut-off low pressure system will track from southwest Idaho\nSaturday night to east of Great Falls by Sunday evening. As this\nsystem does this, it will bring widespread light to moderate\nprecipitation to central Idaho and western Montana. Right now, the\nheaviest precipitation being forecast by the NBM extends roughly\n100 miles north and south from a line from Grangeville, Idaho to\nMissoula to Great Falls with a range from 0.60\" to 1.60\" of total\nprecipitation. The mountains within this line could receive 1 to 2\ninches. This upper trough is a bit colder than the recent system,\nso snow levels could fall to around 7000 feet by daybreak Sunday\nmorning. It could feel very wintry in the backcountry which could\nbe dangerous for the unprepared. \n\nA moderating and drying trend is expected by early next week.",
